## What this file does and does not do

Every path listed as security-sensitive in `SECURITY.md` is owned here, and every
path named in either file exists in the tree. `tools/ci/test_ci_checks.py` checks
both directions, so a security-sensitive path with no owner, and a path that is
listed but is not in the tree, are each a failure.

The criterion for the list is written down in `SECURITY.md`.

That sentence is here because `tools/ci/test_ci_checks.py` checks for it: an ownership
file and a security policy that describe the same paths have to say so in the same
words, or one of them drifts and nothing notices.

What this file does not do is enforce anything on its own. An active ruleset on
`main` requires a pull request, forbids deletion and force-push, and permits only a
squash merge — so the *shape* of a change is protected. It does not require a review,
and `require_code_owner_review` is off, because the only possible reviewer is the
repository owner: GitHub does not count the author's own approval, so requiring one
reviewer would make the repository unmergeable, and requiring a team that does not
exist would block every pull request.

That is the whole of the protection, and it is worth reading plainly: a change can
only reach `main` through a pull request, and no human being is obliged to look at it.
The gap is an open external gate in `docs/development/release-readiness.md`, and it
is the reason the next thing this project needs is a second maintainer rather than
another test.
